/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The contact breaker has three problems as engines get faster.
   - Electronic ignition removes the rubbing block.
   - Early modules kept the points' behaviour.
   - td is the dwell time in seconds, D the dwell as a fraction of the spark interval and Ts = 120 ÷ (N × z) the spark interval in seconds, with N the speed in rev/min and z the number of cylinders of a four-stroke engine.
   - Programmed ignition, also called electronic spark advance or digital ignition, stores the spark timing in a table in the ECU memory.
   - The ECU then corrects the mapped value.
   - The chain has these blocks: a crank sensor (speed and crank angle) with a reference mark for TDC; a manifold pressure or air-flow sensor (load); coolant temperature and knock sensors; the ECU with its maps; the igniter; the coil; and, on ol
   - The ECU sends a dwell pulse to the igniter.
   - Four control methods appear in order of increasing freedom.
   - A service technician sees the result as a scan-tool data stream: rpm, manifold pressure, coolant temperature, calculated advance, knock retard and dwell.
*/
