/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Waste spark uses one double-ended coil for two cylinders whose pistons rise and fall together but are 360° apart in the cycle.
   - When cylinder 1 is near the end of compression, cylinder 4 is near the end of exhaust.
   - Waste spark needs no cap, rotor or long leads, so it is cheap and common on small engines.
   - Direct ignition gives every cylinder its own coil, mounted on the plug (coil-on-plug) or close to it with a short lead (coil-near-plug).
   - The high-voltage path is a few centimetres long, so lead losses and radio interference fall.
   - There is no rotor to burn, cap to track, or distributor shaft play to upset timing, and the timing cannot be adjusted by hand.
*/
