/* book exam traps: Saying a waste-spark coil fires once per cycle. It fires once every revolution, twice per 720°, and each firing sparks both of its plugs. | Thinking direct ignition can run on the crank sensor alone. It needs the cam sensor to tell compression from exhaust; waste spark does not. | Pairing cylinders by neighbouring numbers. Pair the cylinders whose pistons move together and are 360° apart in the firing order: 1 & 4, 2 & 3. | Forgetting that the two waste-spark plugs are in series and of opposite polarity; the exhaust-stroke plug takes only a few kilovolts. | Calling coil-near-plug the same as coil-on-plug; coil-near-plug keeps a short high-voltage lead. */
